Task 1 is done: a failed -p run no longer replaces or creates the --output file. It was committed as 163a063e before the interruption. I re-ran the checks on that commit and nothing else needed doing.

What changed: A failed -p run (exit 1) now leaves the --output path as it was, the same way curl -o does. Stderr says so in one of two ways:
• <file> was not written: the run failed, so the existing file was left as it was
• <file> was not written: the run failed, so no file was created

Exit codes are the same as before, and a successful run still replaces the file (the #1214 decision).

Which failures count: I gated the write on one function, run_failed_overall, which treats a run as failed if it hit an error or stopped short of a complete answer (including a refusal, or a cut at an output limit the user never set). The JSON output's is_error field now uses the same function, so the file and that field can't disagree. I didn't reuse emit_output's own is_error parameter because, at the two final call sites, it doesn't cover refusals or those cut-offs.

Checked again just now:
• cargo fmt --check and cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings both pass.
• The 4 tests in tests/output_failed_run.rs pass. They run the real binary and cover: an existing file kept on failure, no file created on failure, the file kept on a refusal, and a successful run that still replaces it.
• The full cargo test run passes: 7636 tests, 0 failures.
• No NEUTERED marker is left in any file the commit touched.
• The commit body has the before- and after-tables, the predicate and the reason for it, and the recall control. Before the fix, 3 of the 4 new tests failed. With the check deliberately disabled, the same 3 failed again; with it restored, all 4 passed.
